# Memory leaks in C (lab 6)

A leak = heap memory that is still allocated but no pointer refers to it any more, so it can never be freed.

Common causes in our lab code:
- returning early from a function after `malloc` without `free` on that path;
- overwriting the only pointer: `buf = realloc(buf, n)` loses the block when realloc fails;
- freeing a struct but not the strings it points to;
- building a linked list and freeing only the head.

## Tools

- `valgrind --leak-check=full ./a.out` reports "definitely lost" and "indirectly lost" blocks with the allocation stack.
- AddressSanitizer: compile with `-fsanitize=address -g`; LeakSanitizer runs at exit.

## Habits

- one owner per allocation; write down who frees it;
- `goto cleanup` pattern for functions with several exits;
- set pointers to NULL after free to catch double frees.

My lab 6 submission had 3 leaks, all in the error paths of the tokenizer. Lost 5 marks. Fixed version is in the labs folder.
